        "content": "\n          <p>The first seventy-two hours following an assault represent a crucial clinical window for protecting your health and preserving forensic evidence:</p>\n          <ol>\n            <li><strong>Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (PEP):</strong> PEP is a 28-day course of antiretroviral medications that prevents HIV from establishing infection in the body. To be effective, it must be started within seventy-two hours of exposure, ideally within the first twenty-four hours. Complete adherence to the medication schedule is essential for maximum protection.</li>\n            <li><strong>Emergency Contraception:</strong> Taking emergency hormonal contraception within seventy-two hours dramatically reduces the risk of unintended pregnancy. Clinical staff can provide guidance on appropriate regimens.</li>\n            <li><strong>Forensic Evidence Preservation:</strong> If you plan to pursue legal justice, avoid bathing, showering, douching, brushing your teeth, or washing your clothes prior to an examination. Place the clothes you wore in a clean paper bag (not plastic, as plastic retains moisture and degrades DNA).</li>\n            <li><strong>Visit a Specialized SARC Clinic:</strong> Sexual Assault Referral Centres (SARCs), such as the Mirabel Centre in Lagos, Tamar SARC in Enugu, or Vivian Centre in Abuja, provide free, confidential medical treatment, forensic documentation, and trauma counseling without requiring police involvement first.</li>\n            <li><strong>Toxicology Sampling:</strong> If you suspect drink tampering or chemical incapacitation, insist on rapid urine and blood collection, as many sedatives are rapidly metabolized within twelve to twenty-four hours.</li>\n          </ol>\n        "
